course: chemistry
lesson: 3
title: Molecules: when atoms join up
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Atoms rarely stay alone: they join up into groups called molecules, a bit like Lego bricks clicking together.
explain: "H2O" means two hydrogen atoms joined to one oxygen atom: that is one molecule of water.
explain: The same few kinds of atom, joined in different ways, give completely different substances: salt and sugar look alike but are made of different atoms.
explain: When atoms change partners, a new substance appears, which is what happens when a nail slowly rusts in water.
explain: No atom is lost in a reaction; the atoms are only rearranged, so a rusty nail actually weighs more than the clean one, because it has gained oxygen from the air.
fact: A molecule is a group of atoms joined together; a water molecule (H2O) is two hydrogen atoms joined to one oxygen atom.
fact: Hydrogen and oxygen are gases on their own, but joined together they make a completely new substance, water.
fact: Your breath carries carbon dioxide (one carbon and two oxygen atoms) and water vapour; the water shows up as mist on a cold window.
fact: Plants take in carbon dioxide and use it to build sugar and wood.
fact: Salt is made of sodium and chlorine; sugar is made of carbon, hydrogen and oxygen.
fact: Sugar burns to black carbon in a hot pan; salt does not burn.
fact: In a mixture the ingredients are just mixed and can be separated; in a compound the atoms are joined and behave like a new substance.
fact: Mixed iron filings and sulphur powder can be separated with a magnet; after a teacher heats them, they form iron sulphide, which the magnet no longer picks out.
fact: Our noses sense particular molecules; natural gas has no smell, so companies add a smelly chemical to make leaks noticeable.
fact: Some molecules come in mirror-image pairs, like left and right gloves, and the two can smell different: one version of carvone smells of spearmint, its mirror twin of caraway seeds.
fact: In ball-and-stick models, balls stand for atoms and sticks for bonds; carbon is usually black, oxygen red and hydrogen white, by custom only.
fact: A water molecule has 3 atoms, a sugar molecule 45, and the DNA in one of your cells has billions; stretched out it would be about two metres long, but it is extremely thin and tightly coiled and packed.
fact: Everything is made of chemicals, including water, apples and you; what matters is which chemical and how much.
fact: A molecule is the same whether a plant or a lab made it, and natural does not always mean safe: the berries of deadly nightshade, a wild plant, are poisonous.
fact: Carbon atoms can link into long chains and rings, which is how huge molecules like sugar, wood and DNA are built.
fact: Rust forms when iron atoms join with oxygen and water, and a rusted nail weighs more than the clean nail.
exercise: What does "H2O" mean? | Two hydrogen atoms joined to one oxygen atom | Two oxygen atoms joined to one hydrogen atom | Hot water at two degrees | Water from two different places
exercise: You breathe onto a cold window and it mists up. What is the mist? | Water from your breath, turned to tiny drops | Carbon dioxide from your breath, frozen solid onto the cold glass | Dust stirred up by your breath | Oxygen that the glass has soaked up
exercise: What happens to the carbon dioxide we breathe out, in a garden? | Plants take it in and build sugar and wood from it | It sinks into the soil and stays there for good, deep under the roots | It floats up and leaves the Earth | It turns back into oxygen by itself
exercise: Salt and sugar look like the same white grains. What is the real difference? | They are made of different atoms joined in different ways | Sugar grains are only bigger salt grains | Salt is just sugar that has been dried out until it lost its sweetness | There is no difference except the taste
exercise: How could you tell salt from sugar without tasting, with a grown-up's help? | Heat them in a pan: sugar browns and burns, salt does not | Look closely to see which one of them is the whiter of the two | Smell them, because salt smells of the sea | Weigh them, sugar is always heavier
exercise: Before heating, a magnet pulls the iron out of an iron and sulphur mixture. Why not after heating? | The atoms joined into a new compound that the magnet no longer picks out | The heat from the flame melted the magnet, so it lost all of its pull for good | The iron burned away completely | The sulphur hides the iron under a coat
exercise: What is the difference between a mixture and a compound? | In a compound the atoms are joined, in a mixture they are only mixed | A compound is always a liquid, and a mixture is always some kind of solid | A mixture is made in a lab, a compound in nature | There is no difference at all
exercise: Why does a gas company add a smell to cooker gas? | So people notice a leak | So the flame burns brighter | So the gas costs less to make | So the gas burns more slowly
exercise: Why can a lemon and an orange smell different? | Each fruit gives off its own particular smell molecules | Lemons are colder than oranges, and cold things smell sharper | Your nose only smells the colour | Oranges have no smell molecules at all
exercise: In a ball-and-stick model, what do the sticks show? | The bonds joining the atoms | Tiny real sticks that are inside every molecule | The paths electrons fly along | Where the molecule is weakest
exercise: How many atoms are in one molecule of water? | Three | Only two | Forty-five | Billions
exercise: Which kind of atom can link into long chains and rings to build huge molecules? | Carbon | Hydrogen | Chlorine | Uranium
exercise: A juice advert says "chemical-free!" What is true? | Everything, juice included, is made of chemicals | Fresh juice really has no chemicals in it at all | Only fizzy juice has chemicals | Chemicals are only found in labs
exercise: A nail sits in water for weeks and rusts. Does it get heavier or lighter? | Heavier: it gains oxygen | Lighter, because rust falls off | Exactly the same, not a gram more | Lighter, because water eats the iron
test: Your friend says: "Water is made of two gases, so it should be a gas too." What is the answer? | Joined atoms make a new substance with new behaviour | Water is secretly a gas that is only pretending to be wet | Water is only part hydrogen, mostly sand | She is right, water is always a gas
test: "Is the carbon in an oak tree from stuff animals breathed out ages ago?" Best answer? | Partly yes, trees build wood from carbon dioxide in the air | No, trees make all their carbon out of sunlight | No, all tree carbon comes up from the soil through the roots | Only if the animals lived in the tree
test: In a cartoon, a lab-made lemon flavour is called "fake poison" though its molecule is exactly the same as the fruit's. What's wrong? | A molecule is the same whether a plant or a lab made it | Nothing is wrong, because molecules made in labs are always poisonous to eat | Only plants can make real molecules | Fruit molecules are always safer
test: Your gran says: "Natural is always safe, artificial is always bad." Put her right. | Some natural things like deadly nightshade are poisonous; what matters is which and how much | She is right, because nature never makes any poisons, only people in factories do | Artificial things are always the safest | Only water is ever safe
test: How does a two-metre DNA molecule fit inside a tiny cell? | It is extremely thin and tightly coiled and packed | It is folded in half only twice | It sticks out of the cell like a long tail | It is cut into pieces and thrown away
test: "my brother says atoms in a ball-and-stick model are really red and white balls" What is the truth? | The colours are only a custom; real atoms are not coloured balls | He is right, oxygen atoms really are red and hydrogen atoms are white | Real atoms are all black | Atoms are white until they join up
test: What would happen if you tried to separate iron sulphide back into iron and sulphur with a magnet? | It would not work, the atoms are joined | The iron would jump straight out onto the magnet | The sulphur would stick to the magnet | The compound would explode
test: A struck match gives a sharp smell. Where does it come from? | New gas molecules made by the burning, which your nose senses | The wood was always smelly inside, and the flame just lets it out | Your nose gets scratched by the flame | Smoke is the only thing with no molecules
test: "rusty nail weighs more?? rubbish, rust is crumbly" Is the claim right? | Yes, the rust has extra oxygen atoms from the air | No, rust always weighs less, because bits of it crumble off | No, rust weighs nothing | Yes, because water makes nails grow
test: A sugar molecule has 45 atoms. Which atoms are they? | Carbon, hydrogen and oxygen | Sodium and chlorine, like salt | Only carbon, packed in a ring | Iron, oxygen and a little sulphur
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises a molecule? | A group of two or more atoms joined together | A single atom deprived of its electrons | A mixture of substances that may be separated by a magnet | A particle visible to the unaided eye
exam: Identify the principal distinction between a mixture and a compound. | In a compound the constituent atoms are chemically joined, whereas in a mixture they are not | A compound is invariably liquid, whereas a mixture is invariably solid | A mixture contains a single element only, whereas a compound contains a great many elements | A compound may be separated by a magnet, whereas a mixture may not
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established facts concerning the assertion that a beverage is "chemical-free". | All substances, beverages included, are composed of chemicals | Beverages from fruit contain no chemicals whatsoever | Only beverages manufactured in laboratories contain chemicals | Chemicals are present solely in cleaning products
exam: It is given that an iron nail is exposed to water and air for several weeks. Ascertain the effect upon its mass. | The mass increases, oxygen having combined with the iron | The mass decreases, iron having been destroyed | The mass remains wholly unaltered | The mass decreases to nothing
